    faqs: [
      { q: "What conversion rate should I expect from Medicare leads?", a: "It depends heavily on format. Exclusive internet leads typically convert 8% to 15%, shared leads 3% to 8%, and live transfers 15% to 25% for an agent with disciplined follow-up." },
      { q: "Does the cheapest lead price mean the best value?", a: "Not necessarily. Calculate cost-per-enrolled-client by dividing total spend by actual enrollments. A higher-priced lead with a strong conversion rate often produces a lower true cost per client than a cheap lead that rarely closes." },
      { q: "How fast should I contact a new Medicare lead?", a: "As close to immediately as possible. Contacting a lead within five minutes of delivery produces significantly better contact and conversion rates than waiting even thirty minutes." },
      { q: "How do I verify a provider's conversion claims before buying?", a: "Ask for references from agents in a comparable market and practice area, then actually contact them and ask specific questions about real conversion experience rather than relying on marketing materials." },
      { q: "Should I expect conversion rates to stay consistent year-round?", a: "Not always. Compare how a lead source performs during peak Annual Enrollment Period volume versus quieter months, since some sources see conversion quality shift meaningfully between seasons." },
      { q: "Why does contact rate matter separately from close rate?", a: "A lead source with strong close rates among contacted prospects can still underperform overall if contact rate is poor due to inaccurate numbers, so tracking both separately gives a fuller picture of true value." },
      { q: "Is it better to buy more leads or improve conversion on current volume?", a: "Improving conversion on current volume typically produces more sustainable growth per dollar spent than simply increasing lead volume without a genuine conversion strategy behind it." },
    ],
